          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance only partly answers your question. It comes from the *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence* document, an infectious diseases and urology society educational summary.\n\n**What it covers:**\n- **Who it applies to:** An uncomplicated infection is acute bladder infection (cystitis) in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. Pregnancy, men, catheters, structural problems, kidney stones, weakened immunity, and suspected kidney involvement are managed on a different pathway.\n- **Diagnosis:** When someone has typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, guidance says treatment can usually start without a urine culture. A urine dipstick adds little in that group. A positive result confirms what is already likely, and a negative result does not reliably rule infection out. Dipstick testing is more useful when symptoms are unclear.\n\n**What it does not cover:** The retrieved passages do not describe treatment choices, treatment length, culture indications, or recurrence advice. Those sections exist in the document but were not returned, so I can't tell you what the guidance says about them.\n\nThe other documents returned were about respiratory infections, diabetes, and influenza, and none address urinary infections.\n\nIf you have urinary symptoms, a clinician can assess them and recommend treatment. Seek care promptly if you have fever, back or flank pain, vomiting, or confusion, since those can signal a more serious infection.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
